#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c4a7d1-90a6-5d23-9fa2-e93bd90263f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{3}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z+1}{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343552} {:answer/id #uuid "6100088c-66a2-5deb-894a-7e581f4dbf65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z+1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343553} {:answer/id #uuid "39260a09-41e3-5270-bd4d-f5b8d759a827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{3}=\\frac{y+5}{1}=\\frac{z+1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343554} {:answer/id #uuid "3c58ff24-0cef-5c21-b9d9-70883af5c10c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343555} {:answer/id #uuid "5c413646-05d6-55ad-87c5-747e4f1de4d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{-1}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z+1}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c58ff24-0cef-5c21-b9d9-70883af5c10c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343555} :answer-field/id #uuid "055c498e-43ab-5b42-9766-32df873a037b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57e97057-e8bc-5a18-a1e1-2b43855aa175" :question/math-academy-id "q-127410" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-2t \\\\ y=-5t \\\\ z=1-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(3,0,1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y-0}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f09e9e-3f23-5403-911e-738d2b9c46bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{-8}=\\frac{y-4}{-3}=\\frac{z-5}{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343559} {:answer/id #uuid "bc74b0cd-f228-5745-8402-f886f256fcfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y+4}{-5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343560} {:answer/id #uuid "29c345eb-080c-5d4d-8336-39f30eeb7f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{-8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343561} {:answer/id #uuid "212c1a75-9740-5370-b2ac-d752a8080b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343562} {:answer/id #uuid "806ffd2b-5edb-58f0-801b-10e7ccac1f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "212c1a75-9740-5370-b2ac-d752a8080b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343562} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b8616c8-9d1c-5552-be85-685b70032d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68f94319-5269-5c99-8ae2-6775bc4441c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127466" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/assets/1647eb5cca32c6fdc99e23507db151a8299b29bfa70ebfbaa941e17fe4638586.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,4,5)$ and $Q(7,-1,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨7,-1,2⟩-⟨-1,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-5,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9640fe34-6da0-52cf-9be1-a13d9cbccd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343566} {:answer/id #uuid "155e553b-f3f1-523a-ba0f-6a59dc2489d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{-4},\\,z=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343567} {:answer/id #uuid "3a979667-d100-5f70-aaec-f521fe120305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-4},\\,z=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343568} {:answer/id #uuid "28d38cad-94b5-5266-8b60-2ced65e75903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343569} {:answer/id #uuid "5466bdda-44a7-522d-ac23-7fefa26806d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28d38cad-94b5-5266-8b60-2ced65e75903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343569} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f89ed3-9f36-5ed7-a075-840c44199506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "660620a9-8a2e-5788-8e01-c31fb36aca66" :question/math-academy-id "q-127527" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/assets/8722b93655e069e2f35147b572c16ed7af408a86f8a88597c79aea8fe59e9c4d.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-3,3⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3.$ For example, our two points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=3.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ part and instead write $z=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9d1adf0-7fc8-5474-a3d4-45d2421830f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y-10}{6}=\\frac{z-3}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343573} {:answer/id #uuid "0b386dad-4a3c-56e4-818f-7510956f92d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y+6}{10}=\\frac{z-2}{3},\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} {:answer/id #uuid "56e4a3e0-599b-529a-8e1f-d05e2098f1bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343575} {:answer/id #uuid "406d740c-121e-538b-afa2-2765c1db1434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343576} {:answer/id #uuid "bccab0f6-7b5b-5364-952d-6bf1f9aca950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y+10}{6}=\\frac{z+3}{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "406d740c-121e-538b-afa2-2765c1db1434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343576} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a074eef-ca0d-5612-93e1-0bf70c937bb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e46ec1f2-b47a-5934-a8b0-4cd40878864d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71035" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6+10t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3\n$$"}]}
